  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 139 AM CDT Fri Sep 4 2026\n\nCentral and northeastern SD and west central MN will be getting a \nlittle bit of a break from the scattered early morning showers and \nstorms today. While there will not be any showers, weaker winds over \nnortheastern SD and west central MN as well as high relative \nhumidity values will help patchy to areas of fog to develop this \nmorning. This fog could cause localized areas of visibilities to \ndrop below 2 miles at times this morning over portions of \nnortheastern SD and west central MN, which should dissipate once the \nsun rises. As this is happen, an upper level ridge will start to \nbuild over SD into the weekend. The flow under this ridge will help \nwarm, moist air to move into central and northeastern SD today and \ninto the weekend. Temperatures today will be about 10-15 degrees \nwarmer than normal, with highs in the upper 80s to mid 90s. Things \nwill warm even more Saturday, with highs in the upper 80s to upper \n90s. With the warm, humid air around Saturday, the heat index values \ncould reach the triple digits in some areas over central SD. \n\nModels are forecasting multiple upper-level shortwaves to move along \nthe top of the ridge over SD. There is slightly lower confidence in \nthe timing of these shortwaves as the models are not in consensus on \nit. While these shortwaves could provide upper-level support for \nshower and storm development, there also needs to be a source of \nlift at the surface. There will be a lee of the Rockies surface \ntrough sitting over western SD and models are hinting at an attached \nwarm front slowly moving in and sitting over the area this afternoon \nand into the weekend. These boundaries will be able to provide some \nof the lift needed for shower and storm development, though the \nstorms will need to overcome a fairly decent cap to have any \nlongevity and/or become severe. This cap is from an inversion due to \nthe warm air moving in aloft. Instability will be building over \ncentral and northeastern SD today, with CAPE values of 1000-2000 \nJ/kg over the area this evening. With this instability, as well as \nthe moisture moving in and the lift from the surface trough and warm \nfront, showers and storms could try to develop late this afternoon \ninto tonight. However, there is lower confidence in this as they \nwould have to be able to overcome the cap. As stuff tries to \ndevelop, it could cause some isolated pulse, pop up showers and \nstorms that could try to get going but are not able to stay up for \nvery long. If anything were able to overcome the cap and become a \nstronger storm, there will be lapse rates of 7-8 C/km and bulk shear \nvalues of 35-40 kts that could help the storm to have some larger \nhail develop, though there is very low confidence that this will \nhappen. \n\nDepending on when the upper-level shortwave move through, these pulsy \nshowers and storms could pop up and move over central and \nnortheastern SD tonight through Sunday. The model soundings show \nthat there is plenty of instability and moisture that sticks around \nthough Sunday to help things develop. The instability will increase \nSaturday afternoon and through the night to be between 1000-3000 \nJ/kg over SD. With steep mid-level lapse rates between 6-8 C/km and \nbulk shear values of 30-35 kts over central and northeastern SD, the \nenvironment will be primed for severe storms to develop with large \nhail and gusty winds Saturday afternoon and into the overnight \nhours. There is a Marginal risk (level 1 of 5) for these storms to \nbecome severe. While these storms will most likely develop along the \nsurface trough in western SD and then move into central SD, there is \nlower confidence in the timing and how far east they will make it do \nto models all varying the outcomes because of how they handle the \nupper-level support from the shortwaves. This will need to have an \neye kept on it to see if models start to come to more of a \nconsensus.",
